    <section class="tamu-about-body__section">
      <h2>Georeferncing Our Collections</h2>

      <h3>What is georeferencing?</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Georeferencing</strong> is the process of aligning a scanned historical map with real-world geographic
        coordinates. Once a map is georeferenced, it can be treated as geospatial data, opening up possibilities like:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Layering a historic map on top of a modern web map</li>
        <li>Overlaying one historic map on another to compare how a place has changed over time</li>
        <li>Transcribing geospatial data from historic maps, such as labels, features, or outlines</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        As a user, you'll most often encounter georeferenced maps in interactive web viewers, where a historic map is
        displayed on top of a modern base map so you can see the two side by side.
      </p>

      <h3>Using Georeferenced Maps</h3>
      <p>Many of Texas A&M's historical maps have been georeferenced using <a href="https://allmaps.org/">Allmaps</a>.
        When a map has been georeferenced, a <strong>Georeferenced Map</strong> tab will appear beneath the image.
        Clicking this tab displays the map superimposed on a modern base map in the Allmaps Viewer, where you can
        adjust the transparency to compare the historic and modern geography.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <img src="/themes/custom/tamu_theme/images/georeferenced.png" title="Example of a Georeferenced Map of Cuba" alt="An example of a Georeferenced Map of Cuba" style="max-width:720px;height:auto;background-color:#800000;padding:.25em">
        <figcaption>An example of a Georeferenced Map of Cuba</figcaption>
      </figure>

    </section>

